def L(i,t,q,s,m,a,tr,f):return {'id':f'ANS-{i:03d}','topic':t,'question':q,'shortAnswer':s,'modelAnswer':m,'assumptions':a,'traps':tr,'followUps':f,'reviewedOn':D,'status':'reviewed-full-v1'}
B=[
L(251,'Project Data','How would you distinguish signal from background?','Define signal and background physically, acquire matched blanks/controls, model distributions and uncertainty, and test whether the excess follows the expected condition, scale and reproducibility.','I would preserve raw data and compare target-on/off, blank, reference or spatial/temporal control conditions under the same acquisition. Background may include detector dark response, substrate, environment, contamination, drift or unrelated physics. Subtraction needs normalization and propagated uncertainty; an excess is not automatically the target. I would vary a discriminating parameter, inspect residuals and repeat independent samples.', ['Signal mechanism and control condition defined','Acquisition and normalization matched'],['Subtracting an unmatched blank','Calling every excess signal'],['Which blank?','How propagate uncertainty?','What drift check?','What parameter discriminates?','What if background varies?']),
L(252,'Project Data','How would you report uncertainty?','Report estimate, units, uncertainty type/coverage, method, contributors, assumptions and whether it changes the conclusion; do not use unsupported significant figures.','I would distinguish repeatability, calibration, sampling and model components, include covariance where relevant, and state whether the value is a standard uncertainty, confidence interval or other quantity. For an early project I would not invent an error bar before data and a measurement model exist. I would show sensitivity to processing choices and identify the next action that most reduces decision-critical uncertainty.', ['Measurand and uncertainty model defined','Raw repeats/calibration information available'],['Giving ± without definition','Treating spread as all uncertainty'],['Type A/B?','Coverage factor?','How covariance enters?','Does conclusion change?','How reduce dominant term?']),
L(253,'Project Reasoning','Which assumption is most fragile?','Choose the assumption whose plausible failure most changes the conclusion, explain evidence for it, and name a test or alternative model; if project facts are unknown, do not guess.','Possible fragile assumptions involve phase purity, uniformity, equilibrium, single mechanism, representative sampling, instrument calibration or causal interpretation. I would rank assumptions by sensitivity and support, then design the cheapest discriminating control. The answer must refer to the actual project and current data. Calling every assumption uncertain avoids the question; selecting one with no consequence is also weak.', ['Actual hypothesis/model and evidence chain known','Alternative outcome is testable'],['Naming a generic assumption unrelated to conclusion','Claiming assumptions are certainly true'],['What if it fails?','Which data supports it?','What control tests it?','Alternative model?','How conclusion changes?']),
L(254,'Project Reasoning','What limiting case can test your model?','Take a parameter to a physically interpretable boundary where the model predicts simple behavior, check dimensions and whether assumptions remain valid, then compare with data or simulation.','Examples may be zero concentration, long time, low field, thin sample or high/low temperature, but the relevant limit depends on the actual model. A useful limit distinguishes algebraic error or competing mechanism and has an accessible observable. I would not select a limit outside the model domain. The current project model is unknown, so only the method—not a specific answer—can be rehearsed safely.', ['Actual model and parameter domain specified','Boundary remains physically meaningful'],['Taking infinity without checking assumptions','Choosing a limit every model shares'],['What prediction?','Do units agree?','Does approximation survive?','How measure limit?','Which alternative differs?']),
L(255,'Project Ownership','Which part required the most judgement?','Name one real decision where evidence was incomplete, state options and criteria, explain your choice and uncertainty, and separate your judgement from supervisor direction.','Judgement may involve parameter range, sample rejection, fit region, control, troubleshooting or next measurement. A strong answer shows what information existed, what risks mattered, what was documented and what result could reverse the choice. If no such decision has happened yet, I would say so and identify the planned decision rather than fabricate ownership.', ['Actual decision record available','Personal and supervisor roles separated'],['Presenting routine button operation as judgement','Taking credit for supervisor decision'],['What options?','Which evidence?','What risk?','Who reviewed?','Would you choose differently?']),
L(256,'Project Communication','Summarise your project in one sentence.','Use one sentence containing the actual system, scientific question, method/evidence and current stage—without an unmeasured result or generic application.','A safe template is: “I am investigating [confirmed system/question] by [confirmed or explicitly planned method] to determine [measurable claim], and the work is currently [truthful stage].” The sentence should not list every instrument or claim importance without mechanism. Because material, method and contribution remain unknown in the current ledger, Mission Viva must leave placeholders rather than invent content.', ['Confirmed project fields available','Planned versus completed wording preserved'],['Turning expected result into conclusion','Using jargon without question'],['What is measurable claim?','Which method?','Current stage?','Your role?','One nontechnical version?']),
L(257,'Project Communication','Give the project motivation in 30 seconds.','State the known problem, specific evidence gap, why resolving it matters and the project objective; stop before methods/results unless needed.','A 30-second motivation should move from context to a bounded unanswered question. It should distinguish scientific importance from possible application and avoid “nobody has done this” without literature. The exact material and gap must come from confirmed project fields and sources. If still unknown, say the motivation is being refined with the supervisor and name the information needed.', ['Actual problem and literature gap confirmed','Objective matches available project scope'],['Generic “important for technology” claim','Inventing novelty'],['What is known?','What gap?','Why now?','Who benefits?','What objective follows?']),
L(258,'Project Applications','What property would matter for a space application?','Begin from a specific space function and environment, then identify the measurable property and required stability; no material property is universally “space suitable.”','Possible properties include optical response, conductivity, emissivity, outgassing, radiation tolerance, thermal stability or strength, but relevance depends on payload, structure, thermal or sensor use. I would connect property to requirement, interface, temperature/vacuum/radiation/mechanical conditions, calibration and lifetime. The actual project material/property is unknown, so a specific application claim is not justified yet.', ['Specific application and environment defined','Actual material property measured or planned'],['Saying “lightweight” without function','Calling one room-temperature value space readiness'],['Which subsystem?','Operating range?','How test vacuum?','What baseline?','What lifetime evidence?']),
L(259,'Project Translation','What would you test before claiming usefulness?','Test the requirement-linked property with controls, repeatability, relevant environment, baseline comparison and failure criteria before moving from possibility to usefulness.','I would define the user/function and minimum acceptable metric, confirm sample identity and calibration, repeat independent batches, and test stability, selectivity or reliability as relevant. A prototype/interface test may be required to show the property survives packaging and operation. Accelerated tests need a justified model. Until then, wording remains “possible application,” not demonstrated utility.', ['Application requirement and actual property known','Representative test environment available'],['Using one characterization plot as usefulness','Moving acceptance threshold after data'],['What is baseline?','How many batches?','Which environment?','What failure criterion?','What prototype?']),
L(260,'Project Safety','What safety hazards exist in this work?','List only hazards confirmed for the actual material, precursors, process and instruments, then pair each with approved controls and stop conditions; unknown hazards remain unknown.','Hazards may involve chemical toxicity/reactivity, dust, heat, pressure, vacuum, gas, laser, high voltage, radiation, cryogenics or mechanical equipment, but naming generic hazards as project facts is unsafe. I would use SDS, institutional risk assessment, training and facility procedures, identify waste/emergency response and never improvise. The ledger currently lacks actual materials/methods, so specific controls cannot be rehearsed as facts.', ['Actual route/reagents/instruments confirmed','Institutional procedure and supervision available'],['Inventing a hazard/control list','Treating PPE as the only control'],['Which SDS?','Engineering control?','Stop condition?','Waste route?','What changed after risk review?']),
L(261,'ISRO Fit','What technical skill can you contribute to ISRO?','Choose one evidenced skill—such as calibrated materials characterization, optics/detectors, electronics/instrumentation or data—and connect it to a bounded mission-system task and learning plan.','I would avoid claiming broad spacecraft expertise. A credible first contribution might be executing a controlled measurement, analysing uncertainty, calibrating a sensor, documenting a test or supporting material/payload validation under supervision. I would cite actual project or practice evidence and one current official role/centre need. The organization determines posting and training; willingness to learn and reliability matter alongside fundamentals.', ['Actual skill evidence available','Current role/centre checked officially'],['Listing every physics topic','Claiming immediate subsystem ownership'],['Which deliverable?','What evidence?','Which centre source?','What training needed?','How validate work?']),
L(262,'Space Career Motivation','Why space technology rather than only astronomy?','Astronomy motivates questions about the universe; space technology adds the challenge of building calibrated, reliable systems that create measurements and services under real constraints.','My answer would not devalue astronomy. Physics, optics, detectors, materials and data connect both: astronomy asks what to observe and infer, while spacecraft/payload work demands interfaces, thermal/power/pointing, qualification and operations. I am interested in turning scientific understanding into dependable measurements and would choose the role based on actual tasks rather than the word “space.”', ['Genuine interests and target role known','No claim that astronomy lacks technology'],['Contrasting fields dismissively','Saying only that space is exciting'],['Which payload task?','How astronomy helps?','Which constraint interests you?','What skill transfers?','Which role would you choose?']),
L(263,'Launch Performance','What determines payload mass to orbit?','Payload capability follows required orbit/trajectory and total delta-v, propulsion performance, propellant/dry mass, staging, structural/aero loads, guidance losses, margins, launch site and mission constraints.','The rocket equation gives ideal stage delta-v from effective exhaust velocity and mass ratio, but real ascent pays gravity, drag and steering losses and must satisfy thrust-to-weight, dynamic pressure, thermal, structural and control limits. Target altitude, inclination and injection accuracy matter. Payload is part of the final mass budget; it cannot be inferred from thrust alone or one vehicle number.', ['Public conceptual launch analysis','Target orbit and vehicle architecture specified'],['Equating payload with maximum lift-off mass difference','Ignoring losses and inclination'],['Rocket equation?','Why staging?','Launch latitude effect?','What is gravity loss?','How margin enters?']),
L(264,'Propulsion','Compare solid and liquid propulsion conceptually.','Solid motors are mechanically simpler and storable with high thrust but limited throttling/restart; liquid systems offer controllability and potentially high performance with tanks, feed, ignition and operational complexity.','A solid stores fuel and oxidizer together in a grain whose geometry drives thrust history; once ignited it is difficult to stop or restart. Liquid propellants are stored separately and fed to a chamber, enabling throttle, cutoff and sometimes restart, but adding pumps/pressurization, plumbing, leakage, slosh and control. Performance, safety, cost and suitability depend on stage and propellant; neither is universally better.', ['Conceptual public-domain comparison','Specific mission/stage context stated'],['Saying solid is always less efficient','Ignoring liquid feed and cryogenic complexity'],['How grain geometry matters?','Pressure-fed versus pump?','Restart value?','Storage safety?','Which upper stage?']),
L(265,'Payload Verification','How would you test a payload?','Trace payload requirements to calibrated component, subsystem and integrated tests across function, interfaces, environment, failure modes and data-product validation.','I would verify optical/RF/sensor performance and calibration first, then power, thermal, data, timing, pointing/alignment and command interfaces. Flight-representative environmental tests may include vibration, thermal-vacuum, EMC and radiation as required, with functional checks before/during/after. Ground processing and reference targets test the measurement chain. Exact levels and procedures come from programme standards and authorized facilities.', ['Payload requirements and configuration controlled','Applicable environment/standards defined'],['Testing only survival, not performance','Calling one bench test qualification'],['Which requirement first?','How calibration transfers?','What interface test?','What anomaly criterion?','How validate data product?']),
L(266,'Spacecraft Budgets','How do mass and power constraints interact?','Power generation/storage hardware adds mass and thermal load, while mass/orbit/attitude choices change available power and mission operation; budgets must be traded by mode with margins.','A higher-power payload may require larger arrays, batteries, regulators, structure and radiators, increasing launch mass and inertia. Mass reduction choices can reduce thermal area or stiffness and create risk. Orbit/eclipse, pointing and degradation shape generation; peak versus average loads shape storage/distribution. I would use a common configuration, avoid double-counting margin and run sensitivity on mission value.', ['Mission modes/orbit defined','Mass, power and thermal budgets use same baseline'],['Treating budgets independently','Using average power only'],['What drives array size?','How eclipse matters?','Thermal coupling?','How margin set?','Which trade test?']),
L(267,'Fault Management','What happens when a subsystem fails?','The system should detect and isolate the fault, protect critical functions, enter a safe or degraded state, recover or reconfigure when justified, and communicate evidence for ground action.','Response depends on criticality, observability, redundancy and autonomy. FDIR must avoid both missed faults and false isolation, and safe mode needs independent enough power, thermal, attitude and communication support. Some failures cause graceful degradation; others are single-point. Tests should include sensor faults, interface loss and incorrect commands under controlled simulation.', ['Specific architecture and failure mode defined','Public non-operational discussion'],['Saying redundancy automatically recovers','Assuming every fault is immediately detectable'],['What is FDIR?','Safe mode needs?','Common-cause risk?','How test fault injection?','When ground decides?']),
L(268,'Space Environment','Why is vacuum a design constraint?','Vacuum removes convection, promotes outgassing/contamination and changes discharge, lubrication, heat transfer and material behavior while pressure loads can act across sealed volumes.','Thermal design relies on conduction and radiation. Volatile materials can deposit on optics; conventional lubricants and adhesives may fail; trapped gas and virtual leaks matter; high voltage can face pressure-transition/discharge issues; electronics cannot cool by air. Vacuum is not “nothing,” and qualification needs material screening, bakeout/cleanliness and thermal-vacuum testing under programme rules.', ['Spacecraft external/internal pressure boundaries defined','Material/electrical operating conditions specified'],['Saying everything cools in vacuum','Ignoring outgassing on optics'],['Why no convection?','What is outgassing?','How lubricate?','High-voltage issue?','How TVAC validates?']),
L(269,'ISRO Awareness','What does ISRO broadly do?','ISRO is India’s national space organization working across access to space, spacecraft/payloads, applications, science and enabling ground/institutional systems; current programmes must be checked officially.','A strong answer is not a mission-name list. I would describe the end-to-end ecosystem—launch, satellites, payloads, communication/remote sensing/navigation/science applications, tracking/control and technology development—then connect one verified centre/work area to my skills. Exact missions, organizational details and recruitment routes can change and should come from the official source ledger.', ['Only stable official profile used','Current examples separately verified'],['Listing stale missions as awareness','Giving only patriotic language'],['Which centre?','One application physics?','Ground segment role?','Where verify current facts?','What skill fits?']),
L(270,'Space Applications','Name a space application and explain its physics.','Choose one verified application, identify the measured electromagnetic or dynamical quantity, trace sensor/orbit/calibration to the user product, and state uncertainty.','For remote sensing, a calibrated detector measures reflected/emitted/backscattered radiation; spectral, spatial, radiometric and temporal resolution plus atmosphere and ground truth shape retrieval. For communication, propagation, antenna gain, bandwidth and noise shape a link. I would pick one and explain mechanism and limitations rather than list applications.', ['Application and sensor/service chosen','Current mission example verified separately'],['Calling satellite images direct truth','Naming application without governing physics'],['What is measured?','Why that orbit?','How calibrated?','Main uncertainty?','What user decision?']),
L(271,'Organization Awareness','How would you discuss a mission you have not followed closely?','Say you are not current on the mission, avoid inventing status or numbers, state only verified broad purpose if known and explain how you would check the official source.','I would separate stable physics from mutable programme facts. If the panel invites reasoning, I can discuss likely measurement chain or subsystem requirements conditionally, while clearly labelling assumptions. I would not bluff launch date, outcome, payload or centre. Afterward I would verify on the organization’s official mission/news page and update a short-expiry current-fact card.', ['No current fact recalled confidently','Official source access identified'],['Guessing mission status','Refusing all reasoning after honest caveat'],['What do you know safely?','Which source?','How reason conditionally?','When stop?','How update notes?']),
L(272,'ISRO Fit','Which ISRO centre or work area interests you and why?','Choose one current officially verified centre/work area, connect its public technical lane to a skill you can defend and remain open to organizational posting needs.','The answer should include what the centre broadly does from the official source, one relevant topic or project method, a realistic first contribution and the skill gap you will train. Bengaluru preference can be stated professionally but not as refusal to relocate. The app’s source ledger is current as of its date, yet it should be refreshed near interview.', ['Centre/work area checked officially','Actual skills and mobility constraints honest'],['Choosing centre only for location','Inventing current projects'],['Which public lane?','What skill evidence?','First deliverable?','What training?','Would you accept other posting?']),
L(273,'Nuclear Science Motivation','Why nuclear science?','Connect interest in nuclear structure/reactions/radiation to a specific peaceful scientific or technology problem, emphasizing measurement, materials, safety, procedure and evidence.','A credible answer may focus on detectors, materials under radiation, accelerators, isotope applications or fundamental nuclear physics. I would state the governing physics and how calibrated experimentation serves the objective, while respecting authorization. Generic prestige, job security or unsafe operational curiosity is weak. Current programmes and roles require official verification.', ['Genuine technical interest selected','Public peaceful context and safety boundary'],['Generic national-service answer only','Claiming operational expertise'],['Which physics topic?','Which measurement?','How project connects?','What safety means?','Which current source?']),
L(274,'Nuclear Materials','How does your materials project connect to nuclear technology?','Connect only confirmed project methods/properties to a public materials need such as characterization, detectors, corrosion or radiation response, and label any application as a hypothesis.','Materials work can transfer through synthesis control, XRD/microscopy/spectroscopy, transport, thermal or mechanical measurement and uncertainty discipline. Nuclear environments add radiation, temperature, chemistry, safety and authorization constraints that the current project may not test. I would identify the gap and next authorized experiment rather than claim nuclear suitability. The actual project material is unknown in Mission Viva.', ['Actual project method/property confirmed','Public nuclear requirement and authorized test context'],['Calling any material nuclear-grade','Inventing radiation data'],['Which property transfers?','What environment missing?','How characterize safely?','What baseline?','What would falsify fit?']),
L(275,'Nuclear Structure','What is binding energy?','Nuclear binding energy is the mass defect between separated nucleons and the bound nucleus times c²; it measures energy required to separate the nucleus under a consistent mass convention.','B=[Zm_p+Nm_n−m_nucleus]c² for nuclear masses, with electron bookkeeping adjusted if atomic masses are used. Binding per nucleon shows broad stability trends but does not alone predict every decay; Q values, separation energies, barriers, conservation laws and matrix elements matter. Volume, surface, Coulomb, asymmetry, pairing and shell effects shape the pattern.', ['Consistent atomic or nuclear masses','Ground/specified nuclear state'],['Mixing atomic and nuclear mass electrons','Using B/A as universal decay criterion'],['Mass defect?','Why B positive?','Separation energy?','SEMF terms?','How Q relates?'])]
